From 1af0edb1b4ce6695a224fe6dacd8ac5ca0603dd0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Timothy J Fontaine <tjfontaine@atxconsulting.com>
Date: Wed, 13 May 2026 11:58:34 -0700
Subject: bifrost: carve the SHMEM principal ring into per-CPU sub-rings
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Goal-roadmap workstream 1. The single 16 MB SHMEM ring with one
producer/consumer cursor pair was the practical throughput
ceiling under hot workloads: every guest CPU CAS-claimed into
the same `producer_pos`, and the compile-profile demo at
FIRE_GAP=0.05 dropped 1.7M records per 16 s window because of
cross-CPU cache-line contention on that single cursor.

This commit carves the 6 MB ringbuf area into N sub-rings of
length `ringbuf_len / N` each, where N = min(num_possible_cpus(),
BIFROST_NUM_CPUS_MAX=16). Producer (BPF program) picks its
sub-ring via `smp_processor_id() % num_cpus`. Consumer (host
renderer) round-robins drain across all sub-rings.

Header layout changes (SHMEM_VERSION bump 3 → 4, breaking — per
project policy we don't care about backward compatibility):

  struct bifrost_shmem_ringbuf_hdr {
      u32 magic, version;
      u64 region_len, ringbuf_off, ringbuf_len;
      u64 btf_off, btf_len, ksyms_off, ksyms_len;
      u64 vma_cache_off, vma_cache_len;
      u32 num_cpus, per_cpu_state_stride;
      u64 per_cpu_ring_len, per_cpu_state_off;
      u8 _pad[24];
      // at offset 128: NUM_CPUS_MAX cache-line-aligned entries
      struct bifrost_shmem_per_cpu per_cpu[BIFROST_NUM_CPUS_MAX];
  };

  struct bifrost_shmem_per_cpu {
      atomic64_t producer_pos, consumer_pos;
      atomic64_t dropped_records, dropped_bytes;
      u8 _pad[32];  // one cache line
  };

`bifrost_shmem_reserve_kernel` picks its CPU's sub-ring up front
(modulo num_cpus on hosts > BIFROST_NUM_CPUS_MAX) and does the
existing atomic CAS + wraparound + padding logic against that
sub-ring's cursors. The wraparound padding record now wraps
within the CPU's sub-ring, not globally.

`bifrost_kfunc_shmem_kick` reads this CPU's producer/consumer
distance instead of the global one — kick fires when this CPU's
sub-ring has fallen behind, not when the aggregate has. Reduces
false-positive kicks across idle CPUs while still firing
promptly on the busy one.

Cross-layer constants pinned:
- drivers/bifrost/wire.rs SHMEM_VERSION=4, SHMEM_NUM_CPUS_MAX=16,
  SHMEM_PER_CPU_STATE_OFF=128, SHMEM_PER_CPU_STATE_STRIDE=64.
- drivers/bifrost/shmem_layout.rs mirrors the same constants.
- Canonical SHA pin in the vendored wire.rs refreshed (host
  canonical is host/bifrost-wire/src/lib.rs).

The host-side DataShmSnapshot and direct-render thread are
updated in a paired host-tree commit; the two layers must land
together because SHMEM_VERSION bumped.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
